The starting cell gives us the color that must be replaced. DFS changes a matching cell first, then checks its four neighbors. Changing the cell immediately also acts as the visited mark.

For example, if the start color is `1` and the new color is `2`, DFS keeps spreading only through connected `1` cells. The input image is changed directly.

```cpp
class Solution{
    void dfs(vector<vector<int>> &image, int row, int col, int oldColor, int newColor) {
        if (row < 0 || row >= image.size() || col < 0 || col >= image[0].size()) {
            return;
        }
        if (image[row][col] != oldColor) {
            return;
        }

        image[row][col] = newColor;

        dfs(image, row - 1, col, oldColor, newColor);
        dfs(image, row + 1, col, oldColor, newColor);
        dfs(image, row, col - 1, oldColor, newColor);
        dfs(image, row, col + 1, oldColor, newColor);
    }

    public:
    vector<vector<int>> floodFill(vector<vector<int>> &image,
                                  int sr, int sc, int newColor) {
        int oldColor = image[sr][sc];
        if (oldColor == newColor) {
            return image;
        }

        dfs(image, sr, sc, oldColor, newColor);
        return image;
    }
};
```

Time complexity: `O(rows * columns)` in the worst case.
Space complexity: `O(rows * columns)` for the recursive call stack in the worst case.

A queue works well here because each pixel can add its four neighbors. The starting color is saved before any painting. A cell is recolored when it enters the queue, so it cannot be added again.

Dry run on `[[1, 1], [1, 0]]`, starting at `(0, 0)` with color `2`:
- Queue starts with `(0, 0)` and that cell becomes `2`.
- Its matching neighbors `(0, 1)` and `(1, 0)` are added.
- Both are painted, while the `0` cell is ignored.

```java
class Solution {
    public int[][] floodFill(int[][] image, int sr, int sc, int newColor) {
        int oldColor = image[sr][sc];
        if (oldColor == newColor) {
            return image;
        }

        int rows = image.length;
        int cols = image[0].length;
        int[][] queue = new int[rows * cols][2];
        int head = 0;
        int tail = 0;

        queue[tail][0] = sr;
        queue[tail][1] = sc;
        tail++;
        image[sr][sc] = newColor;

        int[] rowMove = {-1, 1, 0, 0};
        int[] colMove = {0, 0, -1, 1};

        while (head < tail) {
            int row = queue[head][0];
            int col = queue[head][1];
            head++;

            for (int direction = 0; direction < 4; direction++) {
                int nextRow = row + rowMove[direction];
                int nextCol = col + colMove[direction];

                if (nextRow >= 0 && nextRow < rows &&
                    nextCol >= 0 && nextCol < cols &&
                    image[nextRow][nextCol] == oldColor) {
                    image[nextRow][nextCol] = newColor;
                    queue[tail][0] = nextRow;
                    queue[tail][1] = nextCol;
                    tail++;
                }
            }
        }

        return image;
    }
}
```

Time complexity: `O(rows * columns)`, since each reachable cell is processed once.
Space complexity: `O(rows * columns)` for the queue. The image is modified in place.

Here the image is not used as the visited marker. A separate boolean matrix records which cells were already queued. This makes the state easy to follow, although it uses more memory than changing the image as soon as a cell is found.

Only cells with the original starting color are added. The four direction arrays keep the neighbor checks short.

```go
func floodFill(image [][]int, sr int, sc int, newColor int) [][]int {
    rows := len(image)
    cols := len(image[0])
    oldColor := image[sr][sc]

    if oldColor == newColor {
        return image
    }

    visited := make([][]bool, rows)
    for row := 0; row < rows; row++ {
        visited[row] = make([]bool, cols)
    }

    queueRows := make([]int, 0, rows*cols)
    queueCols := make([]int, 0, rows*cols)
    queueRows = append(queueRows, sr)
    queueCols = append(queueCols, sc)
    visited[sr][sc] = true

    rowChange := []int{-1, 1, 0, 0}
    colChange := []int{0, 0, -1, 1}
    head := 0

    for head < len(queueRows) {
        row := queueRows[head]
        col := queueCols[head]
        head++
        image[row][col] = newColor

        for direction := 0; direction < 4; direction++ {
            nextRow := row + rowChange[direction]
            nextCol := col + colChange[direction]

            if nextRow >= 0 && nextRow < rows &&
                nextCol >= 0 && nextCol < cols &&
                !visited[nextRow][nextCol] &&
                image[nextRow][nextCol] == oldColor {
                visited[nextRow][nextCol] = true
                queueRows = append(queueRows, nextRow)
                queueCols = append(queueCols, nextCol)
            }
        }
    }

    return image
}
```

For a one-cell image, the starting pixel is simply recolored and no neighbor is accepted. The original image is updated directly.

Time complexity: `O(rows * columns)`.
Space complexity: `O(rows * columns)` for the visited matrix and queue.

This recursive version uses a small helper that checks one cell at a time. A cell is accepted only when it is inside the image and still has the starting color. Painting before the recursive calls stops the traversal from coming back to the same cell.

The image is modified in place. The check for equal colors is needed because otherwise every painted cell would still look valid to the recursion.

```javascript
class Solution {
    floodFill(image, sr, sc, newColor) {
        const oldColor = image[sr][sc];
        if (oldColor === newColor) {
            return image;
        }

        const rows = image.length;
        const cols = image[0].length;
        const fill = (row, col) => {
            if (row < 0 || row >= rows || col < 0 || col >= cols) {
                return;
            }
            if (image[row][col] !== oldColor) {
                return;
            }

            image[row][col] = newColor;
            fill(row - 1, col);
            fill(row + 1, col);
            fill(row, col - 1);
            fill(row, col + 1);
        };

        fill(sr, sc);
        return image;
    }
}
```

Dry run: with a start color of `3`, a neighboring `4` is skipped immediately. A neighboring `3` is painted and then checked in the same four directions.

Time complexity: `O(rows * columns)` in the worst case.
Space complexity: `O(rows * columns)` because of the recursion stack.

